    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d744">

                <pb facs="0682" n="676" sameAs="frus:frus1939v04/pg676"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">481.11/138: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Diplomatic Agent and Consul General at
                        Tangier</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Blake</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tangier</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-06-16T12:00:00+00:00">June 16,
                        1939—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received June 16—9:10 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>11. Reference Embassy’s telegram No. 1117, June 13, Residency has replied to my
                    note of June 2 suggesting that delegates meet at Rabat June 23 for examination
                    of claims, leaving to the French and American Governments the adoption or the
                    rejection of the reservations made by the Department.</p>
                <p>Accordingly I am designating Khazen<anchor n="59" xml:id="d744-f1"/> and after a
                    conference with me here he will proceed to Rabat. Above repeated to Paris.</p>
                <p>Please cable Casablanca $80 transportation expenses and per diem for Khazen
                    available for the remainder of current fiscal year.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Blake</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d744-f1" n="59" xml:id="d744-f1-content">
                <p>Michel El-Khazen, interpreter at the Consulate General at Casablanca. Hooker A.
                    Doolittle, First Secretary of the Diplomatic Agency and Consul at Tangier, was
                    also sent to Rabat for these negotiations.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
